{"t":1791081479,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"0822894b-0022-41cb-bba6-6ca531b26e3e","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-04T00:42:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 040042\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n842 PM EDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nMade minor adjustments to the rain chances this evening to \naccount for ongoing light rain in central/northern VA and \nsouthern MD. The rest of the forecast remains on track for\ntonight with rain moving in from south to north late.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A frontal system nearby will maintain rain chances through\n  Sunday with below average temperatures.\n\n- 2) Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to the west of\n  the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal system nearby will maintain rain\nchances through Sunday with below average temperatures.\n\nA stalled front remains across North Carolina this evening with\noverrunning light rain and clouds stretching north to just south\nof I-70/68. Precipitation increases in coverage during the\nsecond half of the night, especially south of I-66 where periods\nof light rain are likely. Some patchy fog is possible, though\nmostly in rural low-lying areas west of the Blue Ridge.\nTemperatures mostly steady in the 50s to around 60F.\n\nA band of light rain, perhaps locally moderate in nature, will \ntrack northeastward across the region through at least the first\nhalf of Sunday. A persistent northeasterly wind should again \nhold temperatures down in the 60s. Any rain chances should \nlargely come to an end just before sundown Sunday. Overnight \nconditions should be fairly similar to tonight with lows again \nin the 50s.\n\nAs a deep upper trough tracks across far eastern Canada, a more\npotent cold front sweeps through the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday. This will favor increasing sunshine, but also a large\nuptick in northwesterly winds. Afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph are possible, but with highs returning to the upper 60s to\nmid 70s (upper 50s to 60s in the mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to\nthe west of the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA strong area of high pressure will build over the area next \nweek, leading to an extended period of dry conditions. As a cooler \nairmass works in and high pressure builds overhead, frost and \nfreezing temperatures may be possible to the west of the Blue Ridge \nboth Monday night and Tuesday night. We'll start to see slightly \nwarmer air build in from the south and west later in the week. High \ntemperatures at lower elevations are forecast to be in the 60s \n(upper 50s/low 60s mountains) on Tuesday, upper 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday (60s mountains), and mid 70s (mid-upper 60s mountains) on \nWednesday and Thursday. Low temperatures will gradually moderate \nfrom the 30s/40s early in the week, to the upper 40s/low 50s later \nin the week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nVFR cloud cover and periods of light rain through the first half\nof the night. The only restrictions now are at CHO with MVFR\nCIGs. Expect conditions to deteriorate as CIGS lower and rain \nincreases during the second half of the night. Likely see MVFR \nthen IFR CIGs spread from south to north after 06Z. This does \ncome with increasing rain chances just before daybreak Sunday.\n\nRain likely continues through the first half of the day before \na drying trend before dark. Low ceilings likely persist into \nSunday night given persistent northeasterly flow. However, VFR \nconditions return for Monday behind a strong cold front. Gusts \nup to 20 to 25 knots are possible during the afternoon hours.\n\nVFR conditions are expected on both Tuesday and Wednesday. \nWinds are forecast to be out of the north on Tuesday, and then \nsouth on Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nEasterly winds have take hold across the waters at around 10-15\nknots. Occasional gusts around 18-20 knots possible over the\ncentral Chesapeake Bay. Expect this to persist into Sunday with\nprevailing winds turning north to northeasterly given the CAD \nwedge in place. The true ramp up in wind fields comes for the \nsecond half of Monday behind a cold front. Small Craft \nAdvisories will likely be needed midday Monday through the \nnight.\n\nSub-SCA level northerly winds are expected on Tuesday. Wind shift \nto out of the south on Wednesday, and are expected to remain sub-SCA \nin nature.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nWater levels will be on the decrease as high pressure builds on\nthe northwest side of the front that continues to push south.\n\nAnother tidal surge is possible going into early next week ahead\nof the next frontal system. Lots of uncertainty at this time,\nbut some minor tidal flooding looks possible, particularly at\nthe more sensitive locations.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/KJP/KRR\nAVIATION...BRO/KJP/KRR\nMARINE...BRO/KJP/KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791081422,"days":{"2026-10-03":{"hi":null,"lo":59,"pop":38,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-04":{"hi":66,"lo":60,"pop":59,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-10-05":{"hi":72,"lo":48,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-06":{"hi":65,"lo":47,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-07":{"hi":70,"lo":52,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":75,"lo":54,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":74,"lo":55,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":72,"lo":null,"pop":6,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791079200,"n":48,"ceil":[4000,3100,3300,2800,2400,1700,1400,1100,900,800,700,700,600,600,500,500,500,500,500,400,400,500,600,800,900,1100,2100,3000,4000,4700,5300,6000,10900,19900,24800,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,nul